FCFS (First Come First Serve)
Definition

FCFS is a non-preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm in which processes are executed in the order of their arrival, i.e., the process that enters the ready queue first is executed first.

Characteristics 
Non-preemptive nature
Once a process starts execution, it continues until completion without interruption.
FIFO queue mechanism
Processes are managed using a First-In-First-Out queue, ensuring strict arrival order execution.
Simple implementation
Easy to design and implement as it requires minimal scheduling logic.
No starvation
Every process gets a chance to execute, so starvation does not occur.
Convoy effect problem
Short processes may wait for long processes, increasing waiting time significantly.
Poor average waiting time
Not efficient for systems with varying burst times, as it increases turnaround and waiting time.
Suitable for batch systems
Works well in environments where response time is not critical and processes are executed sequentially.
SJF (Shortest Job First)
Definition

SJF is a CPU scheduling algorithm in which the process with the smallest burst time is selected for execution first, aiming to minimize average waiting time.

Characteristics 
Selection based on burst time
Process with the shortest CPU burst time is given priority for execution.
Can be preemptive or non-preemptive
Non-preemptive: process runs completely
Preemptive (SRTF): process can be interrupted by a shorter job
Minimum average waiting time
Proven to give the optimal (minimum) waiting time among all scheduling algorithms.
Efficient CPU utilization
Reduces idle time and improves system performance.
Starvation possibility
Long processes may wait indefinitely if shorter jobs keep arriving.
Requires burst time estimation
Needs prior knowledge or prediction of execution time, which is difficult in real systems.
Complex implementation
More complex than FCFS due to sorting and decision-making based on burst time.
